Proposed (22.6s): Use when Go tests are in play: writing or generating `_test.go` files for a package, reviewing or critiquing someone's tests, reworking a bad suite, turning assertions into runnable examples for pkgsite, building a conformance/`httptest`-style testing package for third-party implementers, or shaping an API up front so it stays testable. Also load before finishing any Go code-writing task, since committed code needs contract tests. Applies the maintainer's standard: tests state the package's contract with its users, drafted from the exported docs first; one package-level unit rather than one test per exported symbol; plain-English property names; mandatory representative example; typed errors over string matching; a test that's hard to write means the API needs redesign. Not for non-Go test suites, CI flakiness in other stacks, `go vet`/fuzz triage, or test-plan documents.

